Translation patch: <EdwardsPoint as MultiscalarMul>::multiscalar_mul (src/edwards.rs)

Change: The length sanity check on `scalars.by_ref().size_hint()` / `points.by_ref().size_hint()`
(three `assert_eq!`s on the lower and upper bounds) becomes: collect both inputs into `Vec`s
(nested helper `collect`, scalars first) and `assert_eq!(scalars.len(), points.len())`; the
`Vec`s are passed on to `straus_multiscalar_mul`.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided methods
(`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`, `skip`, `size_hint`, `by_ref`, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
`size_hint` and `by_ref` cannot be called at all, so no rewrite keeps the original
checks. Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the `IntoIter` of a
generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id .. from ExtractBase.Item"),
so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function generic over `It: Iterator`,
called with `x.into_iter()`.

Behaviour: **not identical, same result for valid inputs.** For exact-size iterators of equal length
(the documented contract) both versions return the same point. Differences:
- Panics: the original panics unless both size hints are exact and equal, before consuming
  anything; the patch consumes both inputs and then panics iff the actual lengths differ
  (finite inputs with inexact hints, e.g. `filter`, are now accepted; the message shows
  lengths instead of hints).
- Termination: an unbounded input (e.g. `iter::repeat`), which the original rejected with a
  panic, now makes the function run until allocation fails.
- Order: all scalars are read, then all points; the original backend read all points, then
  all scalars, and interleaved each `borrow()` with its `next()`.
- Zeroization (non-functional): owned `Scalar` items are buffered in a `Vec` that is not
  zeroized (irrelevant for `&Scalar` items and for the Lean model).
No data-dependent control flow is added to this constant-time path.

Facts relied on (not visible in the diff): `straus_multiscalar_mul` is generic over `IntoIterator` with the same `Borrow` bounds and
only iterates its arguments, so passing `Vec<I::Item>` / `Vec<J::Item>` gives it the same
item sequences. The original serial Straus read all points, then all scalars. The impl is
`#[cfg(feature = "alloc")]` (outside the hunk), so `Vec` is available.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

